// Defect: a slice may be stored where it outlives the array it points at.
//
// ref-flow establishes that the compiler does enforce this rule for a plain
// borrowed reference, reporting ErrorInvType "lval outlives the borrowed
// reference you are storing". assignlvalrtype in ir/exp/assign.c reaches that
// check only when both the lval's type and the rval's type are RefTag, so an
// ArrayRefTag on either side skips it and the assignment below is accepted.
//
// The escape is real: after the block ends, 'escaping' names storage that no
// longer exists, and a write through it corrupts the frame. The same hole lets
// a function return a slice borrowed from one of its own locals, which also
// compiles clean today; that case is not annotated here because no diagnostic
// has been established for it, and an expectation naming a code the compiler
// has never produced for that construct would be a guess.
//
// xfail: the annotation names what the reference case already produces, so this
// scenario passes as soon as the check learns about slices.

fn escapingSlice() {
  mut b [3; i32] = [1, 2, 3]
  mut escaping = &[]mut b
  {
    mut inner [2; i32] = [7, 8]
    escaping = &[]mut inner   //~ ErrorInvType "lval outlives the borrowed reference you are storing"
  }
}
